{"plan":{"schema":"whiteboard-plan/1","planId":"wj_f1b61d1111ca4992a8da61231930b78e","revision":1,"goal":"The learner sees how gradient descent uses the slope to take one step downhill, from x = 3 to x = 2.4 on the curve x squared.","audience":"a curious learner","durationSeconds":10,"language":"en","voice":"whiteboard-default-v1","quality":"whiteboard-1080p-v1","sourceRefs":[],"scenes":[{"id":"s1","startFrame":0,"endFrame":300,"learningPoint":"Gradient descent moves against the slope: new x = old x minus step size times slope.","visualIntent":"Axes with the curve y = x squared drawn from 25 points between x = -4 and 4. A dot at (3, 9) with a short tangent line labelled 'slope 6'. An arrow points downhill to a dot at (2.4, 5.76). Handwritten text: 'x = 3 - 0.1 × 6 = 2.4'.","narrationSummary":"At x equals 3, the slope is 6. Step downhill: 3 minus 0.1 times 6 gives 2.4, lower on the curve.","facts":[{"text":"starting x","value":3,"provenance":"claim"},{"text":"height x squared at 3","value":9,"provenance":"computed"},{"text":"slope 2x at 3","value":6,"provenance":"computed"},{"text":"step size","value":0.1,"provenance":"claim"},{"text":"new x = 3 - 0.1 × 6","value":2.4,"provenance":"computed"},{"text":"height x squared at 2.4","value":5.76,"provenance":"computed"}],"illustration":false}],"substitutions":[],"limitations":["Shows a single step only, not the repeated steps that reach the minimum.","Uses a one-variable curve; real models adjust many variables at once.","The step size of 0.1 is picked for illustration."]},"scenes":[{"schema":"whiteboard-scene/1","sceneId":"s1","primitives":[{"id":"curve","kind":"graph","color":"#1a1a1a","width":4,"x":200,"y":120,"w":1000,"h":840,"domain":[-4,4],"range":[0,16],"points":[[-4,16],[-3.667,13.444],[-3.333,11.111],[-3,9],[-2.667,7.111],[-2.333,5.444],[-2,4],[-1.667,2.778],[-1.333,1.778],[-1,1],[-0.667,0.444],[-0.333,0.111],[0,0],[0.333,0.111],[0.667,0.444],[1,1],[1.333,1.778],[1.667,2.778],[2,4],[2.333,5.444],[2.667,7.111],[3,9]